  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nRain will continue to taper off south of the mountains from \nwest to east this morning and afternoon with increased mixing \nresulting in breezy northwest winds. Higher elevations of the\nwestern ME mountains and Coos County NH may see a period of snow\nthrough about noon. \n\nBased on forecast soundings gusts of 20 to 30 mph look \nreasonable, but the top of the mixed layer suggests a few could \napproach 40 mph today. Skies will be cloudy in the morning, but\ndrier air should bring some peeks of sunshine going into the \nafternoon hours, mainly downwind of the mountains. Highs will \nagain be cooler. The mountains and areas to the northwest will \nremain mostly cloudy and cooler with upslope flow and ongoing \nchance for rain and snow showers. \n\nFair and warmer weather is expected on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMean mid-lvl trofing will persist through the middle of this week as \na series of weak s/wv cross over northern New England. This will \nresult in near avg temperatures with daytime highs into the 40s and \n50s from north to south and low temperatures generally at or below \nfreezing. Scattered rain and snow showers will be possible at times \nas these waves of energy cross through, with the majority of this \nactivity over the mtns and towards the Canadian Border. The upper \nlevel pattern will briefly become more zonal on Thursday before a \nmore potent trof arrives Friday into the upcoming weekend. \n\nDuring this timeframe, an area of low pressure is progged to \napproach the eastern seaboard from the center of the country. This \nlow is then likely to strengthen as it moves into a region of \nfavorable jet dynamics and it gains energy/moisture from the \nAtlantic. While there is moderate confidence that an area of low \npressure will be located somewhere near the Northeast late week, \nconfidence in the location and track remains low. For now will just \ncontinue to monitor forecast trends over the coming days to \ndetermine what if any impacts may be felt across the local\nregion.",
